EXAMPLES=[
 {'id':'we-3-source-22','num':'22','title':'Example 22','problem':r'Differentiate (a) \(y=\sinh^{-1}(x^3)\); (b) \(y=\frac{\sinh^{-1}x}{\cosh^{-1}x}\).','given':'Principal inverse hyperbolic functions.','method':'Chain rule in (a); quotient rule in (b).','steps':[r'(a) Set u = x³, so u′ = 3x². The derivative of arsinh u is 1/√(1+u²).',r'Multiply to obtain y′ = 3x²/√(1+x⁶), for every real x. Its denominator is at least 1.',r'(b) Let N = arsinh x and D = arcosh x. The quotient is defined only for x > 1, because arcosh requires x ≥ 1 and D(1) = 0.',r'On x > 1, N′ = 1/√(x²+1) and D′ = 1/√(x²−1).',r'Quotient rule gives y′ = [D/√(x²+1) − N/√(x²−1)]/D².',r'Split into y′ = 1/[√(x²+1)arcosh x] − arsinh x/[√(x²−1)(arcosh x)²].',r'An independent reciprocal-product calculation, y = N·D⁻¹, gives N′/D − ND′/D², agreeing with the same two fractions. The original quotient remains undefined at x = 1.'],'answer':r'(a) \(\frac{3x^2}{\sqrt{1+x^6}}\), all real x. (b) \(\frac1{\sqrt{x^2+1}\cosh^{-1}x}-\frac{\sinh^{-1}x}{\sqrt{x^2-1}[\cosh^{-1}x]^2}\), x > 1.','progressiveSolution':True,'solutionOrigin':'SpaceBook learning enhancement','sourceReferences':[{'page':97,'jpg':'053_page_1.jpg','verification':'complete question and source solution visually compared with JPG and full PDF'}]},
 {'id':'we-3-source-23','num':'23','title':'Example 23','problem':r'Use Maple diff to differentiate: (a) \(x^5+7x+2\); (b) \(\frac{x^4+2x+16}{x^3+3x-2}\); (c) \(x^3+\sin^2x+\operatorname{arccos}x\); (d) \(x^2\cosh x+\operatorname{arcsinh}x\), with respect to x.','given':'Four exact source expressions, each differentiated with respect to x.','method':'Use Maple diff(expression,x), and independently verify the returned derivative.','steps':[r'(a) Enter diff(x^5+7*x+2,x);. Power and sum rules give 5x⁴+7 for every real x.',r'(b) Enter diff((x^4+2*x+16)/(x^3+3*x-2),x);. Set N = x⁴+2x+16, D = x³+3x−2.',r'N′ = 4x³+2 and D′ = 3x²+3. The derivative is (4x³+2)/D − N(3x²+3)/D², for D ≠ 0.',r'(c) Enter diff(x^3+sin(x)^2+arccos(x),x);. The three derivatives are 3x², 2sin x cos x and −1/√(1−x²).',r'Add to obtain 3x²+2sin x cos x−1/√(1−x²), with −1 < x < 1 for a finite real derivative. The original arccos function also has endpoint values.',r'(d) Enter diff(x^2*cosh(x)+arcsinh(x),x);. Product rule gives 2xcosh x+x²sinh x, and the inverse-hyperbolic term gives 1/√(1+x²).',r'Thus the fourth derivative is 2xcosh x+x²sinh x+1/√(1+x²), for all real x.',r'The textbook’s right-click context menu differentiates each same expression with respect to x and yields the same output. The explicit commands and both source presentations are retained in the lesson; the manual calculations verify them without requiring a Maple installation.'],'answer':r'(a) 5x⁴+7. (b) (4x³+2)/D − (x⁴+2x+16)(3x²+3)/D², D = x³+3x−2 ≠ 0. (c) 3x²+2sin x cos x−1/√(1−x²), |x| < 1. (d) 2xcosh x+x²sinh x+1/√(1+x²), all real x.','progressiveSolution':True,'solutionOrigin':'SpaceBook learning enhancement','sourceReferences':[{'pageStart':97,'pageEnd':98,'verification':'complete questions and both command/context-menu solutions visually compared with JPG and full PDF'}]}
]
PROJECT={'id':'ex-3-project-balloon','qNo':'Project','question':r'Take a spherical balloon or ball and inflate it. Given \(V=\frac43\pi r^3\), find dV/dr and evaluate when r = 3.','steps':[r'Volume V depends on radius r, with r ≥ 0. The requested rate is with respect to radius.',r'Differentiate V(r) = (4π/3)r³ using the power rule.',r'dV/dr = (4π/3)·3r² = 4πr².',r'At r = 3, substitute r² = 9 to obtain dV/dr = 36π ≈ 113.0973 square length units.',r'If radius is measured in a particular length unit, the rate has units volume per radius, equivalent to that unit squared. The source does not specify centimeters or another length unit.',r'Check geometrically: a small radius increase Δr adds an approximately thin spherical shell of volume 4πr²Δr, agreeing with the derivative.',r'If a time rate is also needed, chain rule gives dV/dt = 4πr² dr/dt. No numerical dr/dt is given, so the source task determines dV/dr rather than a numerical volume-per-time rate.'],'answer':r'\(\frac{dV}{dr}=4\pi r^2\). At r = 3: 36π ≈ 113.0973 square length units.','sourceQuestionBlocks':[dict(figure('balloon-source100','Spherical balloon — Project','Original page 100 project balloon illustration'),displayWidth=110)],'progressiveSolution':True,'solutionOrigin':'SpaceBook learning enhancement','solutionStatus':'worked and checked','sourceReferences':[{'page':100,'jpg':'054_page_2.jpg','verification':'full project and its exact illustration visually compared with JPG and full PDF'}]}
